From eb55712a324902057a093d9e8bb0f0b1506af678 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: root <root@ubuntu.localdomain>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 03:07:45 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 08/10] feat(rlharness): POSIX shared memory for state and
 pixels

Layout from 10-P0 3: [0,64) header, [64,192) the 25 tracked fields as u32,
[192,...) 640x480 RGBA.  SHM <name> creates and maps the segment; HASH and STATE
publish the fields, PIXELS and the per-frame readback publish the frame.  The
header carries magic, protocol version, frame number, state hash, sizes and a
validity flag word.

Verified from Python by mapping /dev/shm/v400smoke: 1,228,992 bytes,
magic 0x30303456, version 1, frame 40, header hash equal to the HASH reply,
25 state fields readable (f0=40 f1=0 f14=20048 f24=1), flags=0x3, and the pixel
block containing real frame data.

Important finding for G0.2 item 4 and WP8: the state hash was identical across
two runs but the pixel checksum differed.  The HUD prints a wall-clock fps
counter ("174.00 fps"), so the rendered pixels are time-dependent even when the
game state is bit-identical.  Byte-identical pixel regeneration therefore needs
either a fixed fps readout in harness mode or an agreement that the HUD fps
region is excluded from the comparison.
